    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d36">

                <pb facs="0061" n="53" sameAs="frus:frus1941v04/pg53"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.94/1972: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                            Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-02-27T00:00:00+09:00">February 27,
                            1941—midnight</date>.<lb/>[Received February 27—12:10 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>334. Embassy’s 230, February 14, 8 p.m. In the course of my conversation
                    yesterday with the Minister for Foreign Affairs I said <pb facs="0062" n="54"
                        xml:id="pg54"/> that I wished him to know that everything Dooman had said to
                    the Vice Minister for Foreign Affairs on February 14 had my entire concurrence
                    and approval. As Mr. Matsuoka had received only an oral report of the
                    conversation from Mr. Ohashi I read to him the entire telegram under reference.
                    Somewhat to my surprise he said that he entirely agreed with what Dooman had
                    said. At the Minister’s request I am sending him today for his personal use a
                    copy of the memorandum of conversation.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
